{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 52, :comments ["<b>For you shall see from afar, etc.</b> This is to be understood in the manner of what it says (Deuteronomy 34:1): ״וַיַּרְאֵהוּ ה׳ אֶת כָּל הָאָרֶץ״, and see what I explained there." "<b>And there you shall not come to the land, etc.</b> It is difficult: after it said ״וְשָׁמָּה לֹא תָבוֹא״, what place is there to repeat and say ״אֶל הָאָרֶץ״, etc.? And it may be explained in the manner of their words, of blessed memory (Deuteronomy Rabbah 2:9), that Moses is destined to enter the land together with the Patriarchs in the time to come, and they said in the Zohar (vol. 2, p. 120a) that he will bring Israel into the land when their redemption comes. Therefore, when HaShem said ״וְשָׁמָּה לֹא תָבוֹא״, He was concerned lest the matter be heard as determining for him that entry into the land is withheld forever and ever. For this reason He said ״אֶל הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי נוֹתֵן״, etc. — meaning, now, and not the land that He will give in the future. And see what I explained on the verse (Deuteronomy 3:25): ״אֶעְבְּרָה נָּא״." "<b>End of the parashah of Ha'azinu</b>"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:21:52.704439664Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}